WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.558
Hello, everyone, we're here 
at KubeCon in Barcelona.

00:00:02.558 --> 00:00:04.201
With me is Aparna Sinha.

00:00:04.201 --> 00:00:07.593
She is Director of Product Management
of Kubernetes and...

00:00:08.213 --> 00:00:10.242
- Anthos.
- Anthos, that's right.

00:00:10.242 --> 00:00:12.902
Can you start off telling us 
what Anthos is?

00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:16.128
Sure, Anthos is our hybrid Cloud offering

00:00:16.128 --> 00:00:20.384
that's based on Kubernetes Istio
and our multicluster management

00:00:20.384 --> 00:00:22.415
control plane that's hosted in the Cloud.

00:00:22.415 --> 00:00:23.642
We're here at KubeCon.

00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:25.591
This conference is all about open source.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:27.733
How does GKE relate to open source,

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:30.568
and why is there 
such a big presence of Google here?

00:00:30.568 --> 00:00:31.944
Why the Google presence?

00:00:31.944 --> 00:00:34.052
Google is the originator 
of the Kubernetes project.

00:00:34.052 --> 00:00:37.011
It came from our history with Borg
as you know.

00:00:37.281 --> 00:00:41.474
We've been running containers
in production for 15 years now.

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:45.143
And we're launching billions 
of containers, new containers,

00:00:45.143 --> 00:00:46.793
every week, even today.

00:00:47.132 --> 00:00:49.211
So that's why Google is here.

00:00:49.211 --> 00:00:51.196
We're very, very passionate 
about this project.

00:00:51.196 --> 00:00:54.235
We have hundreds of engineers
and product managers

00:00:54.235 --> 00:00:57.640
that are working on the project 
and have been since the last five years.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.543
As you know it's the fifth anniversary
of Kubernetes, and so we're having

00:01:01.543 --> 00:01:03.350
a birthday celebration here.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.781
It's also the fifth anniversary,
pretty soon, this year, of GKE.

00:01:07.781 --> 00:01:12.214
So GKE was started pretty much 
in parallel with Kubernetes.

00:01:12.214 --> 00:01:16.518
It is our hosted version of Kubernetes
running in Google Cloud,

00:01:16.518 --> 00:01:18.465
Google Kubernetes engine.

00:01:19.715 --> 00:01:23.166
It is the oldest, most mature,

00:01:23.173 --> 00:01:27.489
most stable, and widely used distribution

00:01:27.489 --> 00:01:30.373
and hosted offering 
of Kubernetes in use today.

00:01:30.555 --> 00:01:33.354
So we're very, very excited
about both Kubernetes

00:01:33.354 --> 00:01:37.897
as well as GKE, and they actually have
really built off of each other.

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:42.071
So another component of Anthos
that I'm really interested in is Istio.

00:01:42.071 --> 00:01:43.283
Can you tell me more about that?

00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:49.161
Yeah, as you move to microservices,
you find that you actually have

00:01:49.161 --> 00:01:52.457
a lot of services in your environment,
and you need a good way

00:01:52.457 --> 00:01:54.137
to manage those services.

00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:59.513
And in particular, you want to be able 
to monitor the services,

00:01:59.582 --> 00:02:02.860
have observability about which services
are talking to which other services,

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.191
to be able to set policies 
on those services

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:09.229
from a security perspective,
and to be able to control traffic

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:10.477
when you need to.

00:02:10.477 --> 00:02:14.249
And this is the essential function 
of an SRE inside Google,

00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:16.349
you know, a site reliability engineer.

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:21.007
Developers are not typically the ones
that are making sure that the service

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:24.228
is up and running 
or setting the security policies.

00:02:24.228 --> 00:02:28.615
And with Istio and with Anthos,
in general what we do is we allow

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:31.898
the ability for that separation
of concerns, where you can have

00:02:31.898 --> 00:02:36.035
an SRE or operator-type role
that is doing the service management,

00:02:36.035 --> 00:02:42.166
that is taking care of monitoring,
securing, and ensuring the reliability

00:02:42.166 --> 00:02:45.186
of your services 
through that traffic management.

00:02:45.243 --> 00:02:48.173
So that's the capability
that we provide with Istio,

00:02:48.173 --> 00:02:52.231
and then the combination of that
and Anthos configuration management,

00:02:52.231 --> 00:02:55.725
which lets you, from a single source 
of truth, set policies,

00:02:55.725 --> 00:02:59.958
both configuration policies,
security policies, RBAC,

00:02:59.958 --> 00:03:02.291
quota-- these types of policies--

00:03:02.291 --> 00:03:06.081
across your multicluster environment,
allows you to do that

00:03:06.081 --> 00:03:07.114
from a single pane of glass.

00:03:07.123 --> 00:03:11.499
That's really what enterprises need,
particularly as they have

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:13.433
these multiple environments,

00:03:13.433 --> 00:03:15.447
whether they're 
at different retail locations

00:03:15.447 --> 00:03:18.862
or On-Prem, different data centers
and Cloud,

00:03:18.862 --> 00:03:23.680
the configuration management capability 
and the service management capability

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.918
is how they're able to standardize 
and manage and have that operations team

00:03:28.918 --> 00:03:31.459
that can take care of the environment,
freeing up the developers

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:35.498
to do their coding and take care 
of the business functions

00:03:35.498 --> 00:03:37.359
that their business requires.

00:03:37.359 --> 00:03:40.411
So it sounds like the Anthos ecosystem
is going to provide a lot

00:03:40.411 --> 00:03:42.880
of very awesome functionality
to customers very soon.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.220
So last question is what 
are you looking forward to

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.205
at KubeCon this week?

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.129
Well, I'm looking forward to your keynote.

00:03:51.729 --> 00:03:54.175
That's definitely a big highlight.

00:03:54.799 --> 00:03:57.261
I'm looking forward to meeting
a lot of the ecosystem

00:03:57.261 --> 00:03:58.758
in the community here.

00:03:58.993 --> 00:04:02.473
Another part of Anthos
is our marketplace,

00:04:02.473 --> 00:04:05.426
so we've got a marketplace 
in Google Cloud,

00:04:05.426 --> 00:04:09.134
and what we've done actually 
in our workloads team

00:04:09.134 --> 00:04:12.305
is we've got the application object.

00:04:12.431 --> 00:04:15.311
And we are working 
with a lot of the community here,

00:04:15.311 --> 00:04:18.912
a lot of the ecosystem,
to create containerized forms

00:04:18.912 --> 00:04:22.322
of their applications,
whether it's in the form of operators

00:04:22.366 --> 00:04:26.326
or it's just containers or some other 
package that they want to use

00:04:26.326 --> 00:04:31.181
and then publishing it to the marketplace
in GCP and that marketplace

00:04:31.181 --> 00:04:35.288
is then available to all Anthos customers,
whether it's on GKE

00:04:35.288 --> 00:04:38.293
or it's on GKE On-Prem,
and they can deploy those applications

00:04:38.293 --> 00:04:43.605
out of the gate with support 
from the ecosystem.

00:04:43.764 --> 00:04:47.668
So we're really bringing the open source
ecosystem to Google Cloud users,

00:04:47.668 --> 00:04:50.701
and I think that has 
a multiplicative effect

00:04:50.701 --> 00:04:53.654
and is a big reason to be here.

00:04:54.255 --> 00:05:00.684
It's a big reason to really build on

00:05:00.684 --> 00:05:02.193
what we've done with the community.

00:05:02.343 --> 00:05:03.563
That's sounds awesome.

00:05:03.563 --> 00:05:05.369
Thank you so much for your time, Aparna.

00:05:05.377 --> 00:05:06.456
Thank you.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.849
♪ (music) ♪

